<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oświęcim</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Auschwitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oświęcim is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> Voivodeship of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the confluence of the Soła and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of a Silesian Piast duchy, sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> in 1457. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the German occupiers, who called it Auschwitz, built near it the largest Nazi concentration and extermination camp, where about 1.1 million people were murdered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oswiecim.jpg" alt="Oświęcim" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aneta Lazurek · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:O%C5%9Bwi%C4%99cim_-_Uk%C5%82ad_urbanistyczny_AL05.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
